1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,881
I get about twelve job applications' worth
of real effort in a week. I'm an

2
00:00:04,931 --> 00:00:09,813
international student, so the visa clock
does not pause. This tool decides where

3
00:00:09,863 --> 00:00:14,744
those twelve go — and every week I guess
wrong, I'm guessing with something I

4
00:00:14,794 --> 00:00:17,210
cannot get back.

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:22,820
One sentence of objective: maximise
expected sponsored-interview yield per

6
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:28,430
application slot. What that leaves out is
almost everything that actually matters

7
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:34,040
day to day — referrals, how good my
application is, pay, team quality, and

8
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:39,650
every firm with no filing record and no
job board my scanner can read.

9
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,510
Naming the omissions up front is the
posture of the tool.

10
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,558
The tool checks itself here, too. Chapter
fifteen sets a skip rate target of at

11
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:53,606
least fifty percent — how many companies
should get filtered out before they are

12
00:00:53,656 --> 00:00:58,654
even scored. This run skips only thirty-
one point five percent. That is reported

13
00:00:58,704 --> 00:01:03,702
as a dial, not enforced as a rule, because
tightening it until the number looks right

14
00:01:03,752 --> 00:01:08,750
would just be optimizing the dial instead
of the decision.

15
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:14,170
When I run it on the real public dataset —
thirty thousand three hundred sixty-nine

16
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:19,590
rows — the gate reports that ninety-four
point nine percent of those rows have no

17
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,300
H-1B data at all.

18
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:27,250
The single most important line of code in
this thing is the one that refuses to read

19
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:32,200
a blank cell as a zero. "We have no
evidence" and "we have evidence of no" are

20
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:37,150
different sentences, and only one of them
is true here.

21
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:42,315
The output is not a ranked list. It is a
move: shift one slot from company A to

22
00:01:42,365 --> 00:01:47,480
company B, with an interval on the gain
and a stability percentage.

23
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:52,804
Two of those moves survive almost every
resample — those bars are full. Three are

24
00:01:52,854 --> 00:01:58,128
stubs. The tool is not saying "move a
little." It is saying "I cannot tell these

25
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:00,790
apart from doing nothing."

26
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:05,774
Under the model's own assumptions, the
expected gain band clears zero — about

27
00:02:05,824 --> 00:02:10,758
plus zero point one two responses per
week. That is not the same as being right

28
00:02:10,808 --> 00:02:13,250
about the world.

29
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:18,690
The tool measures its own overconfidence,
too. If you let it re-optimize inside

30
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:24,130
every resample, it reports a gain of plus
zero point one two eight. Held fixed to

31
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:29,570
the one allocation it actually recommends,
the honest number is plus zero point one

32
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:35,010
two. The gap — about six percent — is the
optimizer fitting noise nobody can see.

33
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:40,450
Reporting only the first number would have
been the easier, more flattering lie.

34
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,730
Here is the surprising failure.

35
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:48,446
Intel: thirteen thousand three hundred
eighteen sponsorships. Microsoft: twelve

36
00:02:48,496 --> 00:02:54,162
thousand two hundred twenty-six. Amgen:
one thousand eight hundred eighty-two. All

37
00:02:54,212 --> 00:02:57,020
score Apply. All get zero slots.

38
00:02:57,070 --> 00:03:01,613
Not because they are bad targets — because
their job boards run on software my

39
00:03:01,663 --> 00:03:03,910
scanner cannot read.

40
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:09,054
The bias in this tool is not in the model.
It is in which companies I built an

41
00:03:09,104 --> 00:03:14,198
adapter for. Nothing in the math is wrong,
and the answer is still shaped by a

42
00:03:14,248 --> 00:03:16,770
plumbing decision I made months ago.

43
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:21,600
There were two ways to define fair here,
and they cannot both hold. Parity says

44
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:26,430
every company gets slots in proportion to
how many are eligible. Calibration says

45
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:31,260
slots follow the evidence. Choosing parity
would mean spending real weeks applying

46
00:03:31,310 --> 00:03:36,090
into postings nobody can verify. Choosing
calibration — what this tool does — means

47
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,920
two hundred thirty-eight companies with
deep sponsorship records, and three

48
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:45,750
hundred twenty-two more with thin ones,
get nothing either way. There is no

49
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,580
allocation that satisfies both definitions
at once.

50
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:55,700
It is also fragile in ways nobody usually
tests. One mis-typed cell out of thirty

51
00:03:55,750 --> 00:04:00,820
thousand three hundred sixty-nine —
writing zero point nine five where a

52
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:05,940
percentage should be — removes the top
company's slot.

53
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:11,164
And five of six reasonable values for a
parameter I invented change the whole

54
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:16,388
allocation. It is more sensitive to a
number I made up than to a whole year of

55
00:04:16,438 --> 00:04:18,1000
missing government filings.

56
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:24,300
Then I ask it to execute — to actually
commit the slots. It refuses. Exit code

57
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:29,600
four. Ten blocks. This is the tool's own
recommendation, and the tool will not let

58
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:32,250
me act on it.

59
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:37,858
Six destinations have postings nobody has
checked. Three of the moves are coin

60
00:04:37,908 --> 00:04:43,466
flips. There is no force flag. If I want
to move a slot, I have to type my name and

61
00:04:43,516 --> 00:04:46,270
a reason, and that goes in a log.

62
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:51,380
A deployer needs more than accuracy. They
need to know who is accountable when it is

63
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:56,490
wrong, what the tool cannot see, and what
it refuses to do.

64
00:04:56,540 --> 00:05:01,444
This tool's arithmetic checks out. It is
also reallocating on correlation dressed

65
00:05:01,494 --> 00:05:06,399
up as causation — the data measures
whether the government approved someone

66
00:05:06,449 --> 00:05:11,353
the company had already chosen, not
whether they would choose me. Accuracy was

67
00:05:11,403 --> 00:05:13,830
never the hard part.

68
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:16,500
Here is where I would not trust this tool.

69
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:20,727
One: any claim that moving slots raises my
odds of an interview — it measures a

70
00:05:20,777 --> 00:05:22,840
different thing.

71
00:05:22,890 --> 00:05:28,456
Two: any company missing from the list,
because five thousand one hundred twenty-

72
00:05:28,506 --> 00:05:34,072
six funded firms never even enter the
pool, and hundreds of deep sponsors are

73
00:05:34,122 --> 00:05:36,880
invisible because of an ATS gap.

74
00:05:36,930 --> 00:05:41,133
Three: every "posting is live" value in
this default run, because not one was

75
00:05:41,183 --> 00:05:43,260
actually checked.

76
00:05:43,310 --> 00:05:47,372
The tool that tells me where to send
twelve applications is also the tool

77
00:05:47,422 --> 00:05:51,484
telling me it might be wrong about all
twelve. That's the version I'd actually

78
00:05:51,534 --> 00:05:53,540
use.
